<!-- EXECUTIVE SUMMARY -->
<section class="cs-exec">
  <div class="cs-exec-inner">
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Apparel catalogs are organized around the priorities of the business managing them: collections, seasonal themes, fabrics, fits, and internal product references that help a merchandising team navigate a large, changing range. That logic works for on-site navigation and inventory management. It is not always how a shopper searches.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">A buyer looking for a specific garment searches in product terms: material, cut, occasion. Not a collection name or brand descriptor. When product titles in a Shopping feed are built for internal reference rather than search relevance, there is a gap between what the searcher expresses and what the account can match against. It shows up in impression coverage, in query relevance, and in products appearing alongside traffic that doesn&rsquo;t convert.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">This account had that gap, alongside campaign structure that had grown incrementally without the responsibilities of each campaign being formally defined.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">When we first reviewed this account, the 4.79&times; ROAS looked reasonable on the surface. Google Ads had been managed largely in-house, with occasional freelancer support, but the underlying structure had never undergone a comprehensive audit.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">That's a common position for a brand that's grown quickly and kept the same setup throughout. The account worked well enough that nobody had a strong reason to question it.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">During the first week, we found several issues that were each modest in isolation but meaningful in combination: product titles inherited from the original Shopify build, limited use of first-party purchaser data, broad Search coverage across a wide mix of query types, and overlapping campaign responsibilities that made query coverage and budget governance harder to interpret.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body">Over the following six weeks, we rebuilt the account around clearer campaign roles, stronger product data, tighter Search controls, and verified customer signals. Across the subsequent documented 17-month period, the account recorded a 6.05&times; blended ROAS on AU$124K in managed spend, approximately 26% above the 4.79&times; ROAS recorded during the previous 2024 period. Attributed revenue across the management period totalled AU$755K.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- FINDINGS -->
<section class="cs-findings">
  <div class="cs-findings-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">What the Initial Account Audit Revealed</h2>

    <div class="cs-findings-list" role="list">
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">01</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Campaign responsibilities had become difficult to govern</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Several ad groups and campaigns covered closely related product themes. The issue was not literal self-competition in the auction; it was that query routing, budget interpretation, and performance ownership had become less clear as the account accumulated structures over time. Without a clean framework for which campaign should handle each type of demand, it was harder to evaluate performance or make deliberate allocation decisions.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">02</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Broad match was diluting Search budget focus</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Broad match was reaching a wide mix of generic fashion searches, editorial queries, and informational lookups alongside higher-intent product demand. Even where those searches did not convert, they consumed budget and made it harder to concentrate Search investment around the terms most closely associated with purchase intent.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">03</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Verified purchaser data was not being used as a Performance Max audience signal</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Performance Max was running without the brand's verified purchase history as a Customer Match audience signal. The campaign still had access to its existing conversion and account signals, but one of the brand's strongest first-party data assets had not been made available to Google. In fashion, where browsing behavior can differ materially from purchase behavior, verified customer data is a particularly relevant additional signal.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num">04</span>
        <div>
          <p class="cs-finding-title">Product titles reflected internal collection language, not buyer search terms</p>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Feed titles had been set at launch and never updated. They used internal collection names rather than the product attributes customers search for. A title like "Coastal Edit Relaxed Trouser" did not communicate product type, material, fit, or color, the attributes that appear in high-intent queries. Impression share on the relevant high-intent searches was low.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-mid-hook">
      <p class="cs-mid-hook-text">If ROAS has remained flat for several quarters, ongoing optimization may not be enough. Sometimes the account needs a structural audit, not another round of adjustments inside the same inherited setup.</p>
      <a href="../growth-diagnostic-landing.html#book" class="cs-mid-hook-link">The Growth Diagnostic is designed to identify exactly those kinds of constraints &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

