<!-- WHAT WE CHANGED -->
<section class="cs-interventions">
  <div class="cs-interventions-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2">What We Changed, and in What Order</h2>

    <div class="cs-intervention-list">
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Completed a full account audit</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">During the first week, we mapped campaign responsibilities, Search query quality, product-feed structure, audience signals, and account-level exclusions. The work was prioritized by likely commercial impact: product feed language and audience signals first, Search match-type cleanup second, campaign consolidation third.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Rebuilt the account around three clearer campaign roles</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We consolidated structures that served overlapping purposes and organized the account around Brand Search, Shopping, and Performance Max. We used available exclusions, negative-keyword controls, campaign settings, and query governance to create clearer responsibilities across the three campaign types. DSA campaigns and broad-match groups without supporting conversion evidence were paused.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Tightened Search targeting and expanded query controls</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We reviewed 90 days of search-term data to identify which queries were associated with stronger purchase intent. High-value terms were organized into more deliberate exact and phrase targeting. Editorial searches, informational lookups, and wholesale queries were excluded where the account evidence supported it. Search-term performance was reviewed on an ongoing basis.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Added verified purchaser data as a Performance Max audience signal</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We uploaded the brand's verified purchase history through Customer Match, giving Performance Max an additional first-party audience signal based on customers who had completed a purchase. This gave Google an explicit first-party reference point rather than requiring it to infer purchase behavior from site visits and conversion signals alone.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cs-intervention">
        <div class="cs-intervention-num" aria-hidden="true">05</div>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-intervention-title">Rebuilt product feed titles around buyer search language</div>
          <p class="cs-intervention-desc">We rewrote feed titles for the top revenue-driving SKUs to lead with the attributes customers search for: gender, product type, material, color, and fit. "Women's Linen Wide-Leg Trousers &middot; Beige &middot; Relaxed Fit" rather than "Coastal Edit Relaxed Trouser." After the titles were rewritten, visibility on the targeted high-intent queries improved within the first reporting window.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cs-callout">
      <p class="cs-callout-text">A 4.79&times; account and a 6.05&times; account can look remarkably similar from the outside. The products may be the same. The ads may look familiar. The difference can sit underneath, in how customer data, product feeds, Search queries, campaign responsibilities, and bidding controls are organized. In this account, the most consequential structural work happened early in the engagement. That rebuilt architecture then became the operating foundation for the documented 17-month management period.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

